# R8 rules for the release build (minifyEnabled true).
#
# Read this before loosening anything: most of what a Capacitor app needs is already
# supplied. @capacitor/android ships consumerProguardFiles, so its rules reach this
# build automatically -- including "-keep public class * extends com.getcapacitor.Plugin
# { *; }", which preserves every plugin class and all its members. Firebase (pulled in by
# @capacitor/push-notifications) likewise ships consumer rules covering its reflective
# component discovery. Manifest-declared components, such as
# BackgroundGeolocationService, get keep rules generated by AGP from the merged manifest.
#
# What is NOT supplied: the individual Capacitor plugins declare proguardFiles rather than
# consumerProguardFiles, so nothing in them propagates here. That happens to be harmless
# today only because none of them actually ship any rules -- the referenced files are
# absent or comment-only. Do not assume that stays true after a plugin upgrade.

# --- Reflection entry point: the plugin registry -------------------------------------
# android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.plugins.json names each plugin by its
# fully-qualified class name as a STRING, and Capacitor resolves it with Class.forName at
# startup. If R8 renames any of these, that plugin silently fails to load -- in release
# only, with no compile-time signal.
#
# The core consumer rule above already keeps them, but it is conditional on the class
# being public AND extending com.getcapacitor.Plugin. These explicit keeps are cheap
# insurance against a future plugin that satisfies neither, and they document the coupling
# to capacitor.plugins.json. Keep this list in step with that file.
-keep class com.equimaps.capacitor_background_geolocation.BackgroundGeolocation { *; }
-keep class com.capacitorjs.plugins.app.AppPlugin { *; }
-keep class com.capacitorjs.plugins.camera.CameraPlugin { *; }
-keep class com.capacitorjs.plugins.geolocation.GeolocationPlugin { *; }
-keep class com.capacitorjs.plugins.pushnotifications.PushNotificationsPlugin { *; }
-keep class com.capacitorjs.plugins.splashscreen.SplashScreenPlugin { *; }
-keep class com.capacitorjs.plugins.statusbar.StatusBarPlugin { *; }

# The background-location foreground service. AGP already derives a keep rule from the
# merged manifest; this is belt and braces, because an unloadable location service is the
# one failure here a technician would notice in the field rather than in testing.
-keep class com.equimaps.capacitor_background_geolocation.BackgroundGeolocationService { *; }

# --- Capacitor annotations -------------------------------------------------------------
# Capacitor reads @CapacitorPlugin off each plugin class at runtime, by reflection, for the
# plugin's JS-facing name and its permission aliases (Bridge.getPermissionStates iterates
# annotation.permissions()). Losing it would make getPermissionState return null.
#
# Honest note, so nobody re-derives this the hard way: these rules were added while chasing
# the camera crash on a theory that R8 had stripped these annotations. That theory was
# WRONG -- a dexdump of the failing build showed all seven @CapacitorPlugin annotations
# present with their full permissions arrays, including Camera's. The real cause was the
# optimization pass (see android/app/build.gradle). These rules are kept because keeping
# reflectively-read annotation types is correct regardless, but do not credit them with
# fixing the crash, and do not assume they are load-bearing.
-keepattributes *Annotation*
-keep @interface com.getcapacitor.annotation.**
-keep class com.getcapacitor.annotation.** { *; }

# --- Crash readability ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Without these, every obfuscated stack trace loses its line numbers. The mapping file at
# android/app/build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt is what turns the renamed frames
# back into source; AGP packages it into the AAB so Play deobfuscates automatically.
-keepattributes SourceFile,LineNumberTable
-renamesourcefileattribute SourceFile

# Capacitor's bridge exposes itself to the WebView over @JavascriptInterface. The rule
# keeping those methods lives in proguard-android-optimize.txt, so it is not repeated here.
